[about]
Title=02 - User Interface Concepts
Intro=What FileDir is made of: the folder as a list, the line you hear and the file behind it, Enter and Backspace, dialogs that all work one way, the status line, and where help is.
Setup=FileDir is open on any folder. Nothing needs pressing in this walk; it is listened to.
Homework=Open a folder and name each thing as you reach it: the list, the line, the status line.

[step]

Say=FileDir is one window. The main view is a list box: the folder you are in, one line per file or folder, with the folder's path and its count said as you arrive.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A line is a file: its name, and more when you ask. Alt plus X, Extra Info, shows everything known about it, one field to a line.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Enter on a folder goes in; Backspace goes up a level. Enter on a file opens it in the program Windows has for it; a page of media opens in the Homer Player.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Every dialog is built the same way: a label and its control, Tab between them, Alt plus the underlined letter to jump to one, Control plus Enter for OK from anywhere, Escape to cancel. Text boxes that ask for a name remember your last ten answers.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The status line says the folder, the line, and how many files are tagged. Shift plus Z says it; Z is the bottom of the alphabet, like the line is the bottom of the window.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Help is in four places, and they are the same in every Homer program. F1 opens the guide, the whole program in one document. Shift plus F1 opens the history of changes. Alt plus F1 says the version and offers the newer one if there is one.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The Help menu, F10 then H, Hotel, holds the same three, and Play Tutorials, which plays these walks. And the menus themselves are help: arrow through any menu and the reader says each command with its key and its letter.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Several folders can be open at once, each in a window of its own. Control plus O opens a folder in a new window; Control plus G goes to one in this window. Shift plus F4 says the titles of all the windows; Control plus Tab moves between them.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A zip archive opens like a folder: Enter goes in, the files are listed, and most commands work on them as on files. Only a zip can be created or changed; any archive can be read.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Marks on a line say its kind as you arrow: a backslash after a name means a folder; a greater-than sign before it means it is tagged. The Apostrophe says the current name and whether it is tagged; Shift plus Apostrophe says the folder you are in.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Text boxes that ask for a folder complete the path as you type, the way a browser completes an address, and remember your recent answers; Up and Down Arrows in the box bring them back.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Two settings dialogs. Configuration Options, Alt plus Shift plus C, is where the word processor, the editor and the player are named; Alt plus Shift plus R decides whether Delete recycles or removes.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Help from inside: F1 the guide, Shift plus F1 the history, Alt plus F1 the version, Alt plus Shift plus H the hotkey summary in a window; and the question mark says what is inside the current file -- its kind, its size, its dates.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Sorting is a state of the window. Control plus S sorts by the field you choose -- name, extension, size, date -- and the status line says the order; the same list sorted by date is a different list to the ear, newest first.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Hidden and system files are off by default and a setting turns them on; the count the folder says as you arrive is the count of what is shown.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Dialogs that ask for a file offer three ways to answer: type a path, pick from the recent list with the arrows, or Browse to the Windows file dialog; whichever you use, the box remembers it next time.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Messages are spoken as they happen and shown on the status line: a count after a copy, a name after a rename, Zipped three files after Shift plus Z. If one is missed, Insert plus Page Down, the reader's own key, reads the status line.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A list can be very long -- a Downloads folder of a thousand files -- and FileDir does not read it to you unless asked; Alt plus Shift plus L, Say List, reads the whole list from the current line down, and any key stops it.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Two windows side by side are the file manager's oldest idea: Control plus O opens a second folder, and Shift plus C from one copies the tagged files to the other, with the other's folder offered as the default answer.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Everything in this walk is Windows underneath: a list box, an edit box, a dialog, a status line. FileDir adds what they say and when, which is why your reader's own commands -- Insert plus Up Arrow, Insert plus Tab, Insert plus Page Down -- work on all of it.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=What this walk taught. I say the key; the reader says what it does.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=F1.
Key=
Hear=Guide

[step]

Say=Alt plus X.
Key=
Hear=Extra Info
